   $q$Read the sentences: "The desert tortoise spends most of the year underground. It emerges mainly after rare spring rains. Those rains bring up the wildflowers it eats." Which choice combines the sentences most effectively and correctly?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The desert tortoise, which spends most of the year underground and emerges mainly after rare spring rains that bring up the wildflowers it eats.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"subject-without-main-verb"},{"text":"Spending most of the year underground, rare spring rains bring the desert tortoise out along with the wildflowers it eats.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"dangling-modifier-created"},{"text":"The desert tortoise spends most of the year underground, it emerges mainly after rare spring rains, which bring up the wildflowers it eats.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-splice-created"},{"text":"The desert tortoise spends most of the year underground, emerging mainly after rare spring rains bring up the wildflowers it eats.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The desert tortoise spends most of the year underground, emerging mainly after rare spring rains bring up the wildflowers it eats.$q$, $q$The participial phrase "emerging mainly after rare spring rains" attaches to the main clause and keeps all three ideas in one complete sentence. The choice beginning "The desert tortoise, which" puts every verb inside the "which" clause, leaving "tortoise" without a main verb.$q$, 'draft', null)
