   $q$Which additional finding, if added after sentence 6, would most strongly show that pacing itself, rather than some other benefit of the groups, explains the result?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Most first-time entrants signed up for the race at least six months before the starting date.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"irrelevant-detail"},{"text":"First-timers who ran alone but started as slowly as the groups finished at nearly the same rate as group runners.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Runners in pace groups said they enjoyed talking with others and felt less lonely during the race.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"supports-rival-explanation"},{"text":"The volunteers who led the pace groups had each completed at least ten marathons of their own.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"indirect-detail"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$First-timers who ran alone but started as slowly as the groups finished at nearly the same rate as group runners.$q$, $q$If solo runners who started slowly did just as well, the slow start, not the group itself, explains the higher finishing rate, which is exactly Mthembu's claim. The finding about enjoying company points to a different cause, social support, instead of pacing.$q$, 'draft', '8fa8f357-fefe-5276-8bda-5e42f95a881d')
