-- Passage wg12-writing-with-evidence-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('cb089783-aab3-5f17-8e7f-a0da3a07aa3b', 'wg12-writing-with-evidence-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g12-writing-with-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Draft: Waiting for the Wind$q$, $q$(1) In "Riding the North Wind," entomologist Tavita Faleolo describes how her team attached tiny radio tags to forty dragonflies and tracked them south along the coast each autumn for three years. (2) Many people are surprised to learn that some dragonflies migrate at all. (3) Faleolo found that the dragonflies did not fly a little every day, as her team had expected. (4) Instead, they stayed in one place for several days at a time and then traveled long distances in a single burst. (5) Nearly every long flight began within two days after a cold front passed, when steady winds blew from the north. (6) On those days, tagged insects covered an average of 58 kilometers, compared with fewer than 5 kilometers on calm days.

(7) These patterns suggest that the dragonflies save energy by letting the wind do much of the work. (8) Faleolo supports this idea with a second finding. (9) ____ (10) Faleolo is careful to note that forty insects is a small sample and that her tags could be detected only near receiving towers along the coast. (11) Dragonflies have four wings that can move independently, which makes them skilled hunters. (12) Still, her results offer strong evidence that, for these small travelers, timing a trip well matters as much as flying hard.$q$, 'draft')
